# Code owners.
#
# The owner is the maintainer of this repository. A fork replaces it with its
# own users or teams. GitHub skips owners that do not exist or have no write
# access; a path whose owners are all skipped has no code owner, and no review
# can be required.
#
# Review by a code owner is enforced by the branch ruleset on the default
# branch ("Require review from Code Owners"), not by this file alone.
#
# Matching: the last pattern that matches a path wins, so specific paths come
# after the default. When a line lists several owners, an approval from any
# one of them satisfies the rule.
# Docs: https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-code-owners

# Default owner for every path not matched below.
*                                           @SritharanK

# The paths below repeat the default owner on purpose: they stay owned by the
# maintainers even if the default line above is changed.

# CI workflows, composite actions, repository settings and this file.
/.github/                                   @SritharanK

# Cluster definitions read by Ansible and by the root Argo CD application.
/clusters/                                  @SritharanK

# GitOps layer: root application, ApplicationSets and AppProjects. The
# AppProjects and tenant definitions decide what every team may deploy where,
# so they are listed explicitly.
/argocd-bootstrap/                          @SritharanK
/argocd-bootstrap/templates/projects/       @SritharanK
/bootstrap/                                 @SritharanK
/tenants/                                   @SritharanK

# Admission policies enforced on every cluster.
/helmcharts/system-charts/kyverno-policies/ @SritharanK

# Node provisioning (RKE2).
/ansible/                                   @SritharanK

# Production values: promotion to prod needs an approval from a code owner of
# this path. In a repository with teams, list only the production approvers
# here: listing the maintainers as well would let a maintainer approval alone
# satisfy the rule.
/envs/prod/                                 @SritharanK
